# Copy this file to .env (git keeps .env out of the repository) and fill in what you use.
# Nothing here is required: the site builds and runs without any of it.
# Guide: https://docs.bearminion.com/forms

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile, the spam check for the Contact form -------------------------------------------------------
# The site key is public. Astro reads it when the site is built, so set it where the site is built and rebuild after a change.
# The secret key stays on the server. Set both, or neither.
# To try it, Cloudflare publishes keys that always pass: site key 1x00000000000000000000AA, secret key 1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
# PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Your form handler, src/forms/contact.ts ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whatever the handler reads from `env` (a service key, a webhook URL): add the names you use, and set the same names in your host's settings.
# `npx bearminion add form resend` writes a handler that reads these three:
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# CONTACT_FROM=Website <contact@your-domain.example>
# CONTACT_TO=you@your-domain.example

# --- Production builds -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set this in your host's build environment (not here): the build fails while a Contact form has no src/forms/contact.ts,
# instead of only warning. It cannot check that the host has the glue file or the secrets.
# BEARMINION_FORMS=required

# --- astro dev -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# While `astro dev` runs, /api/contact only prints the message (a dry run). Turn this on to run your handler for real.
# It fits .env.local better than .env, so nobody sends real messages by accident.
# BEARMINION_FORMS_LIVE=on
